            "body": "> won't calling `read` with the same session base name overwrite the previous one? Am I missing something?\n\nIf the name of the session matches the [`config.file`](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/402ee6c6ec8ea44b22330446c8fb4e615fd3953e/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L90-L91), then the [local session](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/402ee6c6ec8ea44b22330446c8fb4e615fd3953e/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L17) is written, which depends on the current working directory. If the directory is different at the time of writing, then local session files are different.\n\n> Write happens before a new session is sourced.\n\nYes, this is the only reasonable way to do this. But writing is done for the currently active session, i.e. \"this session\", i.e. the one [that is recorded in `v:this_session`](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/402ee6c6ec8ea44b22330446c8fb4e615fd3953e/lua/mini/sessions.lua#L589).\n\n---\n\nSo, as far as I can see, the proposed `MiniSessions.read_dir_session` is essentially: `:cd dir`, `:lua MiniSessions.read(MiniSessions.config.file)`. This is not enough to warrant adding a new separate function, I am afraid.",
